<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How to Market a Business Idea</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reviews on general products or services. Interactive: Collaborate on creating guidelines for group work.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write reviews of business innovations and ideas, evaluating their benefits for efficiency (e.g., "This app helps track sales, which can improve customer service.")
                Can collaborate on creating business guidelines identifying product marketing strategies (e.g., "Our guidelines should suggest social media advertising to attract customers.")
                Can develop a collaborative guide for planning a business, assigning sections to each team member (e.g., "I'll be analyzing the market trends, and you will be preparing the social media campaigns.")
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Quick Write" activity. The teacher asks students to think about an innovative product or service they have recently encountered. Students then write down 3-4 sentences describing how they think it's marketed and why they believe it's successful. This activates prior knowledge and immediately engages them in writing about the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a short review of a hypothetical innovative business idea, explicitly integrating target vocabulary (e.g., <i>innovation, strategy, customer base</i>) and grammar (e.g., first conditional). For example: "Let's consider a new eco-friendly app. I might write: 'This innovative app helps users significantly reduce their energy costs. If we launch it with a strategic social media campaign, we will dramatically increase our customer base and visibility.'" The teacher reads the example aloud, highlighting the pronunciation of words like "launch" /lɔːntʃ/ and "costs" /kɔːsts/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key concepts and vocabulary.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean if a business has a strong 'customer base'?" (Expected answer: Many people who regularly buy their products/services.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a product is 'innovative', is it old-fashioned or new and original?" (Expected answer: New and original.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'If we advertise more, we will increase sales,' what kind of sentence is that?" (Expected answer: A conditional sentence, talking about a possibility.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents 2-3 short, authentic online reviews or descriptive texts of innovative business ideas or apps (e.g., from a tech blog, app store reviews, or business innovation websites). These texts should be brief, professional yet accessible, and demonstrate the evaluation of benefits and marketing strategies. They will serve as models for students' own writing. The genre is "online business review/description," register is "professional yet engaging," and format is "short written text."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> "Writes coherent texts about familiar topics of personal or academic interest, providing relevant details and expressing opinions clearly." (Adapted from MEDUCA's general writing competence, aligned with B1.2).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the provided texts and complete the following writing-focused comprehension tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Written):</b> Students write a one-sentence summary for each text, identifying the main purpose of the review (e.g., "This review highlights the efficiency benefits of a new sales tracking app.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Written):</b> Students match specific marketing strategies or innovative features mentioned in the reviews (e.g., "social media advertising," "automation," "customer-focused design") to the benefits described (e.g., "increased customer engagement," "reduced costs," "improved efficiency"). They write down their matches.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> First conditional ("If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers."), Present perfect for innovations ("Local shops have begun using social media to tell more people about their products."), Future continuous ("I'll be analyzing the market trends.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <i>innovation, business, strategy, marketing, customer base, automation, efficiency, costs, app, engagement, launch, implement, increase, improve, reduce, promote, successful, strategic, competitive, customer-focused, impactful.</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Teacher will remind students to practice the pronunciation of the long vowel sound /ɔː/ (e.g., "cost," "launch") and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words (e.g., "renewable," "successful") when reading their sentences aloud during peer checks.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Building):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentence fragments and vocabulary words. They complete sentences, focusing on the target grammar structures.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Conditional:</b> "If we _____ (develop) a unique app, we _____ (attract) a larger customer base."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Present Perfect for Innovations:</b> "Many businesses _____ (begin) to _____ (implement) advanced technology for better efficiency." (Students complete with 'have begun' and 'implement').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Future Continuous:</b> "Tomorrow, I _____ (analyze) the market trends while my team _____ (prepare) the promotional events." (Students complete with 'will be analyzing' and 'will be preparing').</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Paragraph Writing):</b> Students work individually to write a short paragraph (4-5 sentences) reviewing a hypothetical business innovation. The teacher provides a prompt, e.g., "Write a review for an innovative new 'smart garden' system designed for urban areas. Focus on its benefits and potential marketing strategies." Students must incorporate at least one first conditional sentence, one present perfect sentence, and at least three target vocabulary words. The teacher circulates, offering guidance and checking for accuracy in written expression and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Feedback & Revision):</b> Students exchange their written paragraphs from the previous activity with a partner. They read each other's reviews and provide written feedback on a small checklist focusing on: 1. Correct use of target grammar structures. 2. Inclusion of specific vocabulary. 3. Clarity of ideas and suggestions for improvement (e.g., "Your idea is great, but if you add more detail about the customer-focused benefits, it will be even stronger."). This incorporates pragmatic elements of sharing ideas for innovation and showing respect for suggestions.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative and individual writing tasks culminating in a business review and collaborative guidelines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Guideline Creation:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4) to collaboratively write a short "Guide to Writing an Effective Business Review" (3-4 key points). They must use professional language for business ideas and incorporate ideas for marketing strategies, e.g., "Our guidelines should suggest social media advertising to attract customers." This fulfills the objective of "collaborate on creating guidelines for group work." They can assign sections to each member, e.g., "I'll be analyzing the best structure, and you will be preparing the tips for language use."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual Business Review Writing:</b> Each student then individually chooses one of the following hypothetical business innovations (provided by teacher or from a list generated by students earlier) or an idea from their group's discussion:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An AI-powered personal assistant app for entrepreneurs.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A sustainable clothing brand using advanced, renewable materials.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A local online platform connecting artisans with a wider customer base.</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students write a 5-7 sentence review of their chosen business innovation, evaluating its benefits for efficiency, potential customer engagement, and suggesting marketing strategies. They must explicitly use at least two target grammar structures (first conditional, present perfect for innovations, future continuous) and at least five target vocabulary words from the list. The review should demonstrate professional language and consider the group's collaboratively created guidelines.</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency and accuracy in written expression, collaborative interaction for guideline creation, and successful completion of the individual writing task by incorporating all specified linguistic elements and task requirements.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during collaborative work and a rubric for the individual business review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their collaboratively created "Guide to Writing an Effective Business Review" (one per group) and their individual "Business Innovation Review."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (for individual review):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Clarity (30%):</b> Review clearly identifies the innovation, evaluates its benefits for efficiency, and suggests marketing strategies. Ideas are coherent.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (30%):</b> Accurate use of first conditional, present perfect for innovations, and future continuous (at least two structures evident).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary (25%):</b> Appropriate and varied use of at least five target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>innovation, strategy, marketing, efficiency, customer base, launch, promote, improve</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Professionalism & Tone (15%):</b> Uses professional language for business ideas and maintains an appropriate tone for a review.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Written):</b> Students answer the following questions on an index card before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging aspect of writing your business review, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively do you think you used the new vocabulary and grammatical structures to make your review impactful?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection (Discussion or Written):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn today about the key elements of writing an effective review for a business innovation or marketing strategy?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will practicing with the first conditional and present perfect for innovations help you in future academic or professional writing related to business?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully write coherent and detailed business reviews, integrating the target grammar (first conditional, present perfect for innovations, future continuous) and vocabulary effectively?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the collaborative activities for creating guidelines productive? What adjustments could improve group collaboration and individual writing outcomes in the future?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific writing challenges did students face (e.g., sentence structure, vocabulary choice, organizing ideas), and what scaffolds or further practice will be beneficial for the next lesson?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate the knowledge and skills from this lesson (especially writing about business innovations and marketing strategies) to summarize information and work on a larger 21st Century Skills Project, possibly creating a marketing plan or presentation based on their innovative ideas and reviews. The guidelines and reviews written today will provide a strong foundation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>